# `-d ovh` only — the OVH prod-like test box, whose chunk store is Cloudflare R2 (NOT SeaweedFS).
# Read as [.kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.ovh]; .kamal/secrets (base) is NOT read.
# Safe for git: references and commands only, never a raw credential.
#
# ⚠️ SAME VARIABLE NAMES AS THE UNRAID LEGS, DIFFERENT CREDENTIALS. CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY here
# is the R2 token, while .kamal/secrets resolves the same name to SeaweedFS. Sourcing either from
# secrets-common would push one backend's credential to the other — the failure the base file's
# old comment warned about, now prevented by construction rather than by remembering to export
# the right value before typing `-d ovh`.

# Cloudflare R2 token for bucket maker-chunks-prod (endpoint/bucket/region are in
# config/deploy.ovh.yml in clear — only these two are credentials).
CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$(bin/secret CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY 'op://tinkerex/R2/secret access key')

# The R2 Access Key ID. This USED TO BE an `env.clear` entry read through ERB as
# `<%= ENV["CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID"] %>`, which does NOT work from a secrets file: kamal secrets
# populate env.secret lookups, they are not exported into the process ENV that ERB reads. With
# the variable unset, that ERB rendered empty, YAML parsed it as nil, and the whole destination
# refused to load with `env/clear/CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID: should be a string` — so an OVH deploy was
# impossible without a manual export. It is an env.secret entry now (config/deploy.ovh.yml).
CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID=$(bin/secret CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID 'op://tinkerex/R2/access key id')

# SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN — this box never runs LIBRARY_BACKEND=chunk_remote and CHUNK_REMOTE_URL is
# blanked in config/deploy.ovh.yml (the sync accessory runs only on the Unraid host), so this
# credential is never actually SENT from here. Required only because the `sync` accessory
# (config/deploy.yml, Arc D Task 2) declares it as its own secret and `accessories:` is never
# overridden by any destination overlay, so it folds into every destination's merged config —
# verified against Kamal::Configuration.raw_config for all five, not assumed. NOT in secrets-common
# on purpose (§14g; and unlike SECRET_KEY_BASE, which is — see that file's own comment). Fuller
# comment in .kamal/secrets.remote, the one copy that is functionally exercised.
SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN=$(bin/secret SYNC_ADMIN_TOKEN 'op://tinkerex/tinkerex-sync-admin-token/password')
